Yes. The useful version would be structured cultural exchange, not cultural engineering.

War tries to change behavior through destruction and coercion. Exchange tries to change understanding through exposure, reciprocity, and repeated contact. That can be far less destructive, but only if participation is voluntary and neither side is using the exchange as a disguised attempt to erase the other.

A strong design would look like:

\text{contact}
\rightarrow
\text{shared activity}
\rightarrow
\text{mutual curiosity}
\rightarrow
\text{comparison}
\rightarrow
\text{translation}
\rightarrow
\text{retained differences + better coordination}

The key is that both sides should be allowed to remain themselves.

So instead of “make them more like us,” you want:

\text{learn their frame}
+
\text{let them learn ours}
+
\text{find interoperable ground}

That can be done through student exchanges, arts, sports, science, food, language, joint conservation, professional exchanges, games, storytelling, even collaborative problem-solving.

And your hourglass image fits because cultural change is rarely one-way. Flip the hourglass and the grains move, but the glass still constrains the system. Sometimes one culture changes the other; often both change each other.

The most interesting outcome is not homogenization.

It is more mutual intelligibility without less identity.

That is probably the sweet spot.